#Disease Background
Hemophilia B is an inherited bleeding disorder caused by a deficiency or abnormal function of coagulation factor IX (FIX), 
a protein that is important for normal blood clotting. It mainly affects males because the F9 gene is located on the X chromosome. 
Mutations in the F9 gene can reduce the amount or function of factor IX, making it difficult for the blood to form stable clots.
  
#Gene and Normal Protein Function
The F9 gene is located on the long arm of the X chromosome at Xq27.1 and provides instructions for producing coagulation factor IX. 
Factor IX is a clotting protein that participates in the blood coagulation pathway. When activated to FIXa,
it helps activate factor X during coagulation. Factor IX is mainly found in the blood plasma 
after being produced and secreted by liver cells.

#Documented Mutation
The documented mutation analyzed in this study is NM_000133.4(F9):c.676C>T. This mutation changes one nucleotide from C to T and 
results in the protein change p.Arg226Trp (R226W). It is classified as a missense mutation because the nucleotide substitution changes
one amino acid without shifting the reading frame.

#Hypothesis
If the documented F9 c.676C>T mutation is introduced into the wild-type F9 coding sequence, it is expected to change one amino acid
from arginine to tryptophan without shifting the reading frame. In contrast, the artificial one-nucleotide deletion is 
expected to cause a frameshift, change the downstream amino acid sequence, and possibly produce a premature stop codon.

#Methods
The study used Galaxy to analyze the effects of mutations in the F9 gene. The wild-type F9 coding sequence from NM_000133.4 was 
imported into Galaxy and translated using the Transeq tool to obtain the predicted factor IX protein. 
The documented c.676C>T mutation was then created by changing C to T at nucleotide position 676. 
The mutant sequence was translated and compared with the WT sequence based on protein length, reading frame, and amino acid changes. 
An artificial mutation was also created by deleting one nucleotide at c.71 and comparing its effects with the WT and documented mutant sequences.

#Results
The Galaxy analysis showed that the WT F9 sequence contained 1,386 bp and produced a normal 461-amino-acid factor IX protein. 
The documented c.676C>T mutation did not change the reading frame or protein length but changed one amino acid at position 226. 
In comparison, the artificial c.71delT mutation reduced the CDS length to 1,385 bp, caused a frameshift, changed many downstream amino acids, 
and produced a premature stop codon.

