#Artificial Mutation Experiment
An artificial mutation was created by deleting one thymine (T) at nucleotide position c.71 of the F9 coding sequence. The deletion caused a frameshift,
changing the grouping of downstream codons. The translated protein was therefore expected to have a different amino acid sequence after the deletion and
may contain a premature stop codon.
Molecular Interpretation: Gene → Mutation → Protein → Cellular Effect → Phenotype
F9 gene → c.676C>T mutation → C-to-T substitution → CGG-to-TGG codon change → R226W amino acid substitution → possible alteration of factor IX structure/function → 
reduced normal coagulation → impaired blood clotting → prolonged/excessive bleeding → Hemophilia B.
The F9 gene normally produces factor IX, which is needed for normal blood clotting. The c.676C>T mutation changes the codon from CGG to TGG, 
causing arginine at position 226 to be replaced by tryptophan. Although the protein remains 461 amino acids long, the amino acid substitution may affect factor IX function. 
Reduced factor IX function can impair blood coagulation and contribute to the bleeding phenotype associated with Hemophilia B.

#Limitations
This study was limited to computational analysis using Galaxy, so the effects of the mutations were predicted from DNA and protein sequences. 
Laboratory tests were not performed to directly measure factor IX structure or clotting activity. The study also analyzed only one documented mutation
and one artificial mutation, so the findings do not represent all possible F9 mutations. Further laboratory and clinical studies would be needed to confirm
the actual effects of these mutations.

#Conclusion
The results showed that mutations in the F9 gene can produce different effects on the factor IX protein. The documented c.676C>T mutation caused a single 
amino acid substitution from arginine to tryptophan at position 226 without changing the reading frame or protein length. In contrast, the artificial c.71delT mutation
caused a frameshift and premature stop codon. Overall, the study demonstrated how changes in the F9 DNA sequence can affect the factor IX protein and may contribute to
the bleeding problems associated with Hemophilia B.



